/* ===== memory that behaves like a mind =====
   The old rule kept each person's twelve newest memories and dropped the rest, so a betrayal was lost as soon as twelve trivial things had happened after it.
   Now a memory fades the way a real one does: by time, slowed by how much it mattered (emotional memories last, a wound lasts longest), by being recalled (each recall, spaced out, makes it last longer), and by sleep, which replays what mattered.
   As the detail goes the feeling stays: the episode becomes a gist, and the gist leaves an affective residue about the person. Memories are recalled by how strong and how relevant they are, not by how recent, and recall changes them a little. */
const MOR_NEG = /\b(lied|lie|betray|broke|broken|threat|humiliat|insult|ignored|cheat|cheated|blamed|abandon|ashamed|attack|hurt|went after|barreled|shamed|used me|never told|walked past|left me)\b/i;
const MOR_POS = /\b(kept|forgave|forgive|helped|thank|honest|protected|gave me|gave you my vote|somewhere to go|owe you|stood by|respect|trusted|credit|took you at your word)\b/i;
